31. It appears that when the petitioners were expecting a specific guideline for the Mohal Shamil Fisheries as suggested by the Deputy Commissioner and Upazila Nirbahi Officers. The respondent No. 1, all of a sudden, issued the impugned letter by which the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet was asked to give lease of those fisheries by tender. In the order it has been said that the fisheries, whose area is more than 20 acres would be settled by the District জলমহল ব্যবস্থাপনা কমিটি; the fisheries less than 20 acres and more than three acres in area would be settled by the Upazila Committee and the remaining Jalmohals of Mohal Shamil Jolkor, which are less than 3 acres in area would be settled with the villagers for their use by the respective Union Parishad. It appears that the fisheries which were more than 20 acres and the fisheries which were less than 20 acres but more than 3 acres would be given by way of lease by open tender, and only the remaining fisheries, which were under three acres would be settled by the Union Parishad with the villagers. It also appears that in the order it was stated that the Jolkors which were already silted up would be managed as per Nitimala.&nbsp; 

 &quot;A customary right can exist only in relation to the inhabitants of a district and it cannot be claimed in respect of the public at large. The Custom, if established makes the local law of the district and it creates a right in each of inhabitants irrespective of his estate or interest in any particular property.&quot;&nbsp; 

 42. There should not be any doubt that after the partition of Pakistan when Sylhet became a part of the then East Pakistan, all the fisheries including Mohal Shamil Jolkors came under the ownership and control of the then East Pakistan Government, at present Bangladesh Government. From the memo dated 17-7-1964 (Annexure-B) it appears that 175 fisheries were enlisted as big fisheries by the then Government of East Pakistan for lease after the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950 came into force. The people of former Jainta Porgona could have challenged the inclusion of 175 fisheries in the government lists as their right to catch fish as of customary right was infringed by that inclusion. Since the people of the locality accepted the government decision of would not be wise to open a new debate on that issue.&nbsp;
